WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

Every performance figure published by this exercise is measured on one Apple
Silicon laptop, on a model of 5,774,080 parameters over a corpus of 2,185,575
tokens -- which a full run of 10,485,760 token positions crosses about 4.8
times, and which is shaped to session 5's mixture into roughly 30 epochs of
web against 0.41 of an epoch of agentic. The design is ABOUT the mechanics of a
run four orders of magnitude larger in tokens. It was never run at that scale,
and any figure describing that scale is labelled as modelled rather than
measured.

Efficiency figures quoted from the OPUS paper (4.7% overhead) and from
LightningLM (3.2%) are theirs. They are not reproduced here and are not claimed.

WHAT REPLAY PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

Replay reproduces INPUTS exactly: batch ids, token spans, and the hashes of the
token block, loss mask, position ids and segment ids. It does NOT reproduce
losses, selector scores, gradient norms or checkpoint weight hashes -- floating
point reduction order differs across devices and thread counts -- and those are
reported against a stated tolerance rather than asserted equal.

NO EVALUATION CONTENT IS PUBLISHED

Evaluation and validation shards are registered so the system can refuse them,
never so they can be read. The firewall is deliberately two-sided: the shard
carries a never-train tag AND the trainer independently asks the registry before
consuming anything.

NO DATASET IS REDISTRIBUTED

This exercise fetches openly-licensed corpora, verifies each licence at fetch
time from the source itself, and refuses anything that declares none. It does not
host, mirror or redistribute any dataset. Links point at the publishers, and each
dataset's own licence governs its use.
